body_clean:
Environment:
Puppeteer version: >= 14.4.0
Node.js version: v18.3.0
npm version: 8.11.0
What operating system are you seeing the problem on?: macOS

Bug description:
Hi, at the moment, there is no way to access the close code when the WebSocket connection is terminated. I created this issue to ask if you would be willing to accept a PR which adds this feature.

Here's my use case. I have a man-in-the-middle WebSocket server between the Puppeteer client and a pool of browsers. The server can indicate errors by sending a close frame with a close code and a message. It looks like `ProtocolError: Protocol error (Browser.getVersion): Target closed.` on the client side and there is no way to get to the close code.

I propose that we add the fields `closeCode` and `closeMessage` to the protocol error so that the user can access them like ``catch(e) { console.log(`close code: ${e.closeCode}, message: ${e.closeMessage}`) }``. I would be happy to submit a PR. Please let me know if you would accept it. 🙏 

BTW on Puppeteer < 14.4.0, I am able to access the close code and message by using a custom transport and getting `transport._ws._closeCode` and `transport._ws._closeMessage`. This workaround stopped working in 14.4.0 as the `_ws` field [was made private](https://github.com/puppeteer/puppeteer/compare/v14.3.0...v14.4.0#diff-156fd83564b3b5a1f8dcc7f5759bd6508ac773efbad8fcea4563422d2760b83bR35).

Steps to reproduce the problem:

1. Run the following script using puppeteer >= 14.4.0.

```js
const puppeteer = require("puppeteer");
const {
  NodeWebSocketTransport,
} = require("../node_modules/puppeteer/lib/cjs/puppeteer/node/NodeWebSocketTransport");

(async () => {
  const browserWSEndpoint = "ws://127.0.0.1:9222/devtools/browser/b90a8030-dc57-480e-ae86-5fdaf68cecc6" // change to your was endpoint
  const transport = await NodeWebSocketTransport.create(browserWSEndpoint);
  try {
    const browser = await puppeteer.connect({ transport });
    await browser.close();
  } finally {
    console.log(`closeCode: ${transport._ws._closeCode}, closeMessage: ${transport._ws._closeMessage}`);
  }
})().catch(console.error);
```
2. There is no way to access the close code. The script fails with `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading '_closeCode')`
3. Run the script again with Puppeteer < 14.4.0. The close code and message (empty) are printed out. `closeCode: 1006, closeMessage:`

